description:
FILED s519b (Opus 5.5, s520, 2026-10-18) from the Capture-Tiny one-file acceptance after #3370 (s519a) + #3376 (s519b): 13-stdout-tied.t / 14-stderr-tied.t / 23-all-tied.t now RUN TO THEIR END (744/129, 744/129, 747/130 -- were 631/208, 661/212, 560/284), so their LAST rows are reached for the first time and FAIL:
  13 #873 / 14 #873 / 23 #875 #876  "STDOUT is still tied" / "STDERR is still tied"  -- got undef, expected 'TieLC=GLOB(0x1)'
(t/13-stdout-tied.t:53 `is( tied *STDOUT, $orig_tie, "STDOUT is still tied" )` after the run_test loops.)

PRE-EXISTING, not s519b's: a copy of the dist with 13-stdout-tied.t cut to `run_test("capture")` only fails the same row on main fd04c048 (scratch/s519b/base29) and on the s519b tree.  NOT reproduced outside the dist: a script that ties STDOUT to TieLC and runs capture / capture_stdout / capture_stderr / capture_merged / capture { system } / tee / tee_stdout / tee_merged keeps `tied *STDOUT` == the tie object on PCL as in perl (scratchpad tie3.pl / tie4.pl).  So the untie needs something the dist's t/lib adds: `save_std(qw/stdout/)` (a `>&STDOUT` dup taken BEFORE the tie), Cases.pm's run_test wrappers, or `binmode($builder->failure_output, ':utf8')`.  Next step: bisect t/lib/Cases.pm's run_test body in a copy (scratch/s519b has the method: copy the dist, shrink the test, run `tools/run-dist-t.pl --rows COPY t/13-stdout-tied.t`).

SECOND OBSERVATION, same files: the TAP layer's trailing plan line ("1..873", the file says `plan 'no_plan'`) is NOT printed on main or on the tree, after t/lib/Utils.pm restore_std re-opens STDOUT (`_open \*{uc $h}, ">&" . fileno($fh); close $fh;`).  On the s519b tree (#3346 moved the plan check into an END block) the END block additionally reports `Error in END block: #<sb-sys:fd-stream for "descriptor 1"> is closed` on stderr.  A plain `open my $fh, ">&STDOUT"; open STDOUT, ">&".fileno($fh); close $fh; print` is fine on both (scratchpad dup1.pl), so the closed fd-1 stream is again t/lib-specific.  perl's Test::Builder writes through its OWN dup of STDOUT taken at load, so a program that re-opens STDOUT never loses the plan line: the TAP layer should hold its own handle the same way (the bridge #3346 documents the TAP layer as the builder).

BAR: the four rows pass or are attributed to a minimal perl-vs-PCL reproducer; the plan line printed after a STDOUT re-open (a Pl/t row: Test::More no_plan, re-open STDOUT by dup, the TAP trailer still reaches the original fd).


=== PART 2 DONE (s521c, Opus 5.5, 2026-10-18) = #3530 ===
The no_plan trailer after restore_std: Test::More's TAP now goes through the layer's own dup of fd 1 (s521c member 2).  One-file runs: 13-stdout-tied.t 872/1 PLAN 873, 14-stderr-tied.t 872/1 PLAN 873, 23-all-tied.t 875/2 PLAN 877 -- the trailer is printed, the END block is quiet.

=== PART 1 MEASURED, NOT FIXED (s521c) -- the reproducer and why the one-line fix is unsafe ===
BISECT (a copy of the dist, 13-stdout-tied.t cut to run_test("capture")): run_test as a no-op -> "still tied" PASSES; run_test with ONLY _set_utf8 / _restore_layers (the capture call removed) -> FAILS.  So the untie is t/lib/Cases.pm's `binmode(STDOUT, ":utf8") if fileno(STDOUT)` on the TIED handle.

REPRODUCER (scratch/s521c/pr/tiebin.pl, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL on 68aeebf5 + s521c):
    package TieB;
    sub TIEHANDLE { my $c = shift; my $fh = \do { local *HANDLE }; bless $fh, $c; open($fh, $_[0]) if @_; $fh }
    sub FILENO  { fileno($_[0]) }
    sub BINMODE { print STDERR "BINMODE($_[1])\n"; binmode($_[0], $_[1]) }
    sub PRINT   { my $s = shift; my $fh = $s; print $fh lc join '', @_ }
    package main;
    tie *STDOUT, 'TieB', ">&=STDOUT"; my $t = tied *STDOUT;
    binmode(STDOUT, ":utf8") if fileno(STDOUT);
    print STDERR (tied(*STDOUT) == $t ? "still tied" : "UNTIED"), "\n";
perl: BINMODE(:utf8) / still tied.      PCL: BINMODE(:utf8) / UNTIED.

MECHANISM: the BINMODE method's own `binmode($_[0], ...)` acts on the tie object's `>&=STDOUT` handle, whose stream is descriptor 1; %p-binmode-impl sees a standard descriptor (%p-std-descriptor) and calls %p-std-rebuild 1, which makes a NEW STDOUT stream and re-registers 'STDOUT.  A handle tie is keyed on the STREAM (**p-handle-tie-table**), so the record stays on the old stream and `tied *STDOUT` answers undef.  (Also not perl's: perl's `>&=` handle is a separate PerlIO handle, and its binmode does not change STDOUT's layers; PCL's rewrites *p-std-efs*.)

THE TRIED FIX AND WHAT KILLED IT: carrying the tie record from the old stream to the new one in %p-carry-autoflush (beside $|, which already travels) makes the STDOUT reproducer and 13-stdout-tied.t pass (873/0), but 14-stderr-tied.t (the same file for STDERR) then HANGS past 120 s even cut to one run_test (19 s without the change).  Probable cause, not yet measured: after the rebuild the tie object's inner `>&=STDERR` handle resolves to the SAME new standard stream, so the carried tie makes the inner handle tied too and TieLC's PRINT (`print $fh ...`) recurses.  The root is that PCL keys a handle tie on the STREAM where perl keys it on the GLOB (docs/tie-aggregates.md §7): two names that share a stream share a tie.  Do NOT retry the carry without first separating the `>&=` handle's stream from the standard handle's (or keying the tie on the glob).
